WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.400
Picture an engineer chasing an error on

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.500
a checkout page.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.320
The logs are in one system.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.570
The ticket is in another.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.690
The database schema is in a third.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.580
Six browser tabs,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.320
copy,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.100
paste,

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:16.820
repeat.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.500
And the agent in the terminal can

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.120
only see local files.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.380
A brilliant colleague,

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.690
chained to a desk.

00:00:23.465 --> 00:00:25.045
The Model Context Protocol,

00:00:25.045 --> 00:00:26.375
or MCP,

00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:27.285
is the fix.

00:00:27.285 --> 00:00:29.235
Think of it as USB for agents.

00:00:29.385 --> 00:00:31.705
An MCP server is a small program

00:00:31.705 --> 00:00:34.425
that exposes tools over one standard plug.

00:00:34.425 --> 00:00:36.405
The agent calls them like any other

00:00:36.405 --> 00:00:36.785
tool,

00:00:36.785 --> 00:00:38.355
and the server translates.

00:00:38.565 --> 00:00:40.265
Someone builds a Stripe server,

00:00:40.265 --> 00:00:43.155
and every compatible agent can use Stripe.

00:00:43.851 --> 00:00:44.881
Why it matters:

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.891
an agent that only sees the repo

00:00:46.891 --> 00:00:48.881
is missing most of its context.

00:00:48.971 --> 00:00:49.991
The bug report,

00:00:49.991 --> 00:00:50.901
the logs,

00:00:50.951 --> 00:00:52.891
the schema and the docs all live

00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:53.851
somewhere else.

00:00:53.851 --> 00:00:55.231
Without integrations,

00:00:55.231 --> 00:00:56.921
you become the human middleware,

00:00:56.951 --> 00:00:58.641
shuttling text between tabs.

00:00:58.671 --> 00:00:59.391
With them,

00:00:59.391 --> 00:01:01.061
you go from clipboard to captain.

00:01:01.903 --> 00:01:03.843
Building your own server is small work.

00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:04.693
But remember,

00:01:04.963 --> 00:01:06.583
the model never sees your code.

00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:07.943
It only sees what you wrote about

00:01:07.943 --> 00:01:08.463
it.

00:01:08.463 --> 00:01:10.233
So name tools as verbs.

00:01:10.263 --> 00:01:11.733
Describe every parameter.

00:01:11.983 --> 00:01:14.323
Return errors that say what went wrong,

00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:16.293
so the model can correct itself.

00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:17.933
Keep each tool focused,

00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:19.453
and limit how much it returns.

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:21.989
Tools let the agent act.

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:22.919
Resources,

00:01:22.919 --> 00:01:24.459
like a database schema,

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:25.489
give it the map.

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:27.209
And skills add the know -how.

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:29.719
A folder with a skill .md file

00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:32.179
that describes how your team actually investigates

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:32.879
a problem.

00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:34.579
The server is the hands.

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:36.209
The skill is the training.

00:01:37.023 --> 00:01:39.143
Every integration raises the stakes.

00:01:39.143 --> 00:01:41.403
An agent with database access can delete

00:01:41.403 --> 00:01:41.903
data,

00:01:41.903 --> 00:01:43.703
so make read -only a fact,

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:44.713
not a promise.

00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:47.083
One reference Postgres server claimed to be

00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:47.903
read -only,

00:01:47.903 --> 00:01:50.163
and a crafted query simply stepped around

00:01:50.163 --> 00:01:50.563
it.

00:01:50.563 --> 00:01:52.703
Put the guarantee in the database itself,

00:01:52.703 --> 00:01:54.393
a role that cannot write.

00:01:55.126 --> 00:01:56.776
Then there's integration creep.

00:01:56.786 --> 00:01:59.366
Every connected tool loads its definitions into

00:01:59.366 --> 00:02:01.306
context before you type a word,

00:02:01.306 --> 00:02:03.486
and a model facing a hundred tools

00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:04.956
makes worse choices.

00:02:05.186 --> 00:02:06.586
Curate per project.

00:02:06.586 --> 00:02:08.186
If you wouldn't keep a tab open

00:02:08.186 --> 00:02:09.136
to that system,

00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:10.386
the agent doesn't need it,

00:02:10.386 --> 00:02:12.846
and often a familiar CLI does the

00:02:12.846 --> 00:02:13.816
job for free.

00:02:14.558 --> 00:02:15.868
Start with your database,

00:02:15.958 --> 00:02:17.438
through a read -only role,

00:02:17.438 --> 00:02:18.838
then your ticket tracker,

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:19.948
then monitoring.

00:02:19.998 --> 00:02:22.008
The agents are already capable.

00:02:22.078 --> 00:02:23.858
The question is whether they can see

00:02:23.858 --> 00:02:24.978
enough of your world.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.098
But every connection also lets the world

00:02:27.098 --> 00:02:27.918
talk back.

00:02:27.918 --> 00:02:28.798
Next up,

00:02:28.798 --> 00:02:30.308
the agent attack surface.
